WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.329
Atomics do not automatically solve lifetime safety.

2
00:00:02.329 --> 00:00:10.646
A thread may safely read an atomic flag and still access an object that has already been destroyed if ownership and shutdown were not designed

3
00:00:10.646 --> 00:00:10.978
together.

4
00:00:10.978 --> 00:00:12.642
Publication is a common hazard.

5
00:00:12.642 --> 00:00:20.959
If one thread stores a pointer, reference, or readiness flag for another thread to observe, the design must ensure that the pointed-to object is

6
00:00:20.959 --> 00:00:26.614
fully initialized, visible under the intended ordering, and alive for the entire period readers may use it.

7
00:00:26.614 --> 00:00:31.272
Shared ownership can help in some designs, but it is not a universal answer.

8
00:00:31.272 --> 00:00:35.596
It changes lifetime management and may introduce its own coordination and cleanup questions.

9
00:00:35.596 --> 00:00:38.923
Raw pointers and captured references require even more explicit boundaries.

10
00:00:38.923 --> 00:00:40.586
Background workers need clear ownership.

11
00:00:40.586 --> 00:00:47.573
A detached worker that watches an atomic stop flag may still outlive the buffer, logger, queue, or owning object it uses.

12
00:00:47.573 --> 00:00:52.563
Joining or cooperative cancellation gives the owner a chance to synchronize shutdown with object lifetime.

13
00:00:52.563 --> 00:00:56.222
Shutdown flags are review-sensitive because they coordinate behavior across threads.

14
00:00:56.222 --> 00:01:01.212
The flag should be paired with notification, wake-up, cancellation, or join policy as appropriate.

15
00:01:01.212 --> 00:01:07.533
A thread that never checks the flag, or checks it only after a long wait, may still stall cleanup.

16
00:01:07.533 --> 00:01:08.864
Object destruction order matters.

17
00:01:08.864 --> 00:01:15.185
If an atomic is part of an object, readers must not continue using it after the object is destroyed.

18
00:01:15.185 --> 00:01:20.175
If the atomic points to other state, that other state needs its own lifetime guarantee.

19
00:01:20.175 --> 00:01:23.502
The safe habit is to design synchronization and lifetime together.

20
00:01:23.502 --> 00:01:29.490
Atomic visibility answers what a thread can see; ownership rules answer whether the thing being seen still exists.

21
00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:34.480
Both answers must be true at the same time for the design to be reliable.
